{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent Crusader ruler and military leader during the Middle Ages. He was born in the early 12th century and his life unfolded against the backdrop of the ongoing Crusader states in the Levant. Raymond was the son of Pons Count of Tripoli and his wife Cecile of France the daughter of King Philip I of France. His lineage made him part of the powerful House of Toulouse which had significant influence in the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Accession**\nRaymond inherited the County of Tripoli upon the death of his father Pons in 1137. At the time he was just a teenager which placed him under the guardianship of William of Bures the uncle of his mother. William served as regent and guided Raymond through his early years as ruler. The County of Tripoli was one of the most important Crusader states strategically located at the crossroads between the Muslim world and the Christian states of the Levant. Raymond’s inheritance came at a critical time with the Crusader states facing numerous challenges including internal strife and external pressures from Muslim powers.\n\nRaymond III was also known for his military prowess and diplomatic skills. He played a crucial role in defending the Crusader states against various adversaries. One of his most notable achievements was his involvement in the Siege of Damascus in 1148 alongside the armies of the Second Crusade. Despite the ultimate failure of the crusade Raymond’s leadership was recognized and he continued to be a key figure in the region’s political and military landscape.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Family and Marriage**\nRaymond’s personal life was marked by significant political alliances. In 1153 he married Hodierna of Jerusalem the daughter of Fulk of Jerusalem and Melisende of Jerusalem. This marriage strengthened the ties between the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem two of the most powerful Christian states in the Levant. Hodierna herself was a formidable figure and played an active role in the governance of Tripoli after Raymond’s death. Their union produced several children including Bohemond III who succeeded Raymond as Count of Tripoli.\n\nRaymond’s family connections extended beyond his marriage. He was a cousin of the famous Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos through his mother Cecile. These ties were important in the complex web of alliances and rivalries that characterized the medieval Mediterranean.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Death and Legacy**\nRaymond III died in 1152 after a brief illness. His death occurred during a period of relative stability for the Crusader states though challenges remained. He was succeeded by his son Bohemond III who continued his father’s legacy and played a significant role in the region’s history. Raymond’s contributions to the defense and governance of the County of Tripoli earned him a place among the notable rulers of the Crusader period.\n\nThe County of Tripoli under Raymond’s rule remained a vital buffer against Muslim expansion and a key player in the political dynamics of the Levant. His leadership and strategic mind were crucial in maintaining the independence and security of the Crusader states during a turbulent era. Raymond’s life and reign highlight the complexity and importance of the Crusader states in medieval history.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.29494979977607727}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00038413776201196015}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01811952330172062}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4808386564254761}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.10021013766527176}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -1.2508610486984253}, {"